rows=[
('Item handler dispatch and fallback values','The complete ItemHandlers module defines symbolic handler tables separately from Item FieldUse/BattleUse metadata. UseFromBag uses its own registered callback first; otherwise an existing UseInField callback is converted to1/0, absent both0. ConfirmUseInField absent returns true; UseInField absent returns -1, otherwise truthiness becomes1/0. UseOnPokemon absent false; MaxUses absent or USE_MULTIPLE_STAT_ITEMS_AT_ONCE disabled returns1, otherwise max(callback,1). CanUseInBattle absent true. BattleUseOnPokemon/Battler absent false, otherwise callback result is passed through. Handler existence, metadata eligibility, registration and eventual effect return remain separate gates.',[unit(U,4,116)]),
('Battle item reservation, consumption and cancellation failure prefix','pbRegisterItem writes UseItem,item,target,move into the choice before consuming from the bag. Only is_consumable? items are removed; player removal failure raises after choice writes, enemy removal deletes its owner-list occurrence. Returning an unused consumable adds to player bag only when capacity allows, otherwise raises; enemy owner items are pushed. pbCancelChoice returns any UseItem first, then unregisters Mega and clears choice. A refund failure therefore prevents later unregister/clear; there is no transactional rollback. pbItemUsesAllActions? is the PokeBall predicate.',[unit(A,29,75),unit(Q,5,24)]),
('Battle item execution rechecks and callback result boundary','Pokemon eligibility rejects nil/eggs, then Embargo, then Hyper Mode except scents. At execution party/battler/general-use branches show the use message before rechecking CanUseInBattle with showMessages false; if that gate succeeds they invoke their effect callback then clear choice item, without using the effect callback return value to decide refund. A false recheck displays no effect and returns the reserved item. PokeBall execution calls its handler then clears choice without the same recheck. Exceptions keep the actual preceding reservation/message/effect prefix; no universal ensure-refund is present.',[unit(A,5,27),unit(A,77,148)]),
('BattleUse metadata to actual menu target and command receiver','Scene item menu filters bag entries to battle_use>0; Use requires its selected Use command. BattleUse1 chooses party member,2 additionally chooses move,3 battler,4 opposing battler/target screen,5 current user with no separate target; cancellation returns to the prior menu or closes bag. Selected party indices are remapped through party order, and nil/egg targets are rejected before yielding. Battle command receiver additionally requires BattleUseOnPokemon for1/2 and BattleUseOnBattler for3, checks eligibility, then CanUseInBattle before registering/reserving. Unknown use types yield false. Bag cursor state is saved/restored on the normal tail; callbacks or native failures need not reach that tail.',[unit(S,196,326),unit(Q,100,138)]),
('Command-phase ordering for cancelled choices','Command phase clears choices only for battlers allowed to receive commands and resets nonnegative reserved Mega slots before player then opponent loops. Normal player-menu backtracking cancels the previous battler through the refund/unregister/clear path; unsuccessful submenus cancel the current choice. Fight-menu back/shift unregister Mega and target cancellation returns false. Forced Encore/Struggle and Palace interception follow their explicit preceding gates. A choice already committed before an exception is not generally undone; AI action-selection policy and every submenu native implementation are not proved by this complete command source.',[unit(Q,1,265)]),
('Evolution-stone field-use actual producer and effect prefix','The UseOnPokemon conditional handler is selected by Item.is_evolution_stone?. It rejects Shadow, then calls the individual item-evolution check with the selected item. A target starts/evolves with cancellation disabled/ends the evolution screen within the fade wrapper, refreshes party annotations/screen when that scene type applies, then returns true. No target shows no effect and returns false. Quantity is not used by this handler. Normal outer item-use consumption depends on that true return; a native/evolution exception may interrupt before refresh/return/consumption with preceding individual writes retained.',[unit(E,386,410),unit(U,644,752)]),
('Trade creation, metrics and party commit order','pbStartTrade increments trade statistics before taking the outgoing party member. For an existing incoming Pokemon it reuses and mutates that object and replaces its foreign owner; for a species key it constructs at outgoing level and later resets moves. Nickname/obtain_method2/record_first_moves precede the scene. Trade scene bitmap binding occurs before each selected species_data.apply_metrics(front index1); incoming Pokedex registration/ownership precede animation. Normal wrapper invokes Start/Trade/End then assigns incoming to the original party slot last. Exceptions may leave statistics/owner/Pokedex/evolution writes without final party replacement. No reception rejection selector is silently added to this bare entry.',[unit(X,27,54),unit(X,171,243)]),
('Trade contextual evolution and double-end native boundary','Trade End disposes the message window, fades/disposes sprites and disposes viewport before checking incoming.check_evolution_on_trade(outgoing), and performs a found evolution with cancellation disabled. The conditional newly-owned Pokedex branch invokes End(false) inside Trade; wrapper still invokes End normally. No ended guard suppresses the second invocation. This proves two static call sites, not guaranteed second-check reachability: repeated viewport.dispose host behavior is unobserved. WP32 section6 X01-X03 explicitly retains that boundary as WP32-N02; no new finding follows. Trade predicates and held-item consumption remain governed by the already-read evolution registry and successful post-evolution hooks.',[unit(X,157,243)]),
('Walking producer, happiness and step callback order','Normal pbOnStepTaken increments the 31-bit step count, snapshots repel-active, triggers player-step then transfer callbacks, returns if handled, otherwise checks a wild battle unless eventTriggered/in-menu, and clears encounter_triggered only at the normal tail. Forced route/map interpreter paths trigger only the generic step event then return. The walking happiness callback increments a separate counter; at128 it independently gives each able party member a50percent happiness chance, then resets counter. Exceptions may leave incremented counters or skip resets; no universal post-step cleanup is promised.',[unit(W,1,220)]),
('Actual step-to-encounter producer and prefix','pbBattleOnStepTaken requires an able party, possible encounter tile and a chosen encounter type, then its trigger predicate. It writes global encounter_type before choosing species/level and emitting on_wild_species_chosen. Only allow_encounter? success enters ordinary or double WildBattle.start with can_override true; second double species has its own callback. On normal allowed-battle return encounter_type is cleared and encounter_triggered true; force_single_battle false is written at method tail. Cancellation/override and failures preserve their actual reached prefix, not an unconditional clear. Direction-change callbacks can call this entry directly when not in menu.',[unit(W,172,237)]),
('Partner load actual receiver and commit boundary','pbRegisterPartner resolves TrainerType first and cancels vehicles before pbLoadTrainer. It emits on_trainer_load with the returned trainer before iterating its party to assign foreign owner, recalculate stats and heal; global partner tuple is committed last. Missing trainer or callback failure can stop before that final assignment and does not undo prior vehicle/callback changes. The same default Trainer/TrainerType construction and final-form consumers apply; this producer alone does not prove every partner battle/storage mode.',[unit(W,637,652)]),
('Item acquisition display and bag side effects remain distinct','ItemBall, ReceiveItem and BuyPrize resolve the Item object and quantity names, use move name for machines, and obtain pocket text through the item pocket setting. Their bag-add checks and user messages occur in their explicit per-method order; capacity failure does not grant receipt. Literal Item Name/NamePlural/PortionName/PortionNamePlural and Move Name actual getters therefore feed these display paths, separately from the compiler localization collection already frozen in ELR-I03. This finite fact does not grant all localized corpus pairs, bag capacity implementation, audio assets or native window success.',[unit(W,656,750)])
]
